//? Title: Binary GCD: Exact Integer Reduction with Shifts and Subtractions
//? Subtitle: Stein's invariant, canonical execution traces and full-width checks
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given two nonnegative integers, find their greatest common divisor, for example
to reduce a fraction to lowest terms. Both inputs can span the full unsigned
64-bit range, and zero needs an explicit convention. Binary GCD removes powers
of two and subtracts odd operands, avoiding division. We derive the invariant,
work through 54 and 24, retain the original 1071 and 462 example, and compare
generated cases with an independent reference. The report uses execution states
recorded by the same core loop that computes the public function's answer.
)

\toc

> The method

For positive integers, common divisors satisfy three useful identities:

$$
\gcd(2a,2b)=2\gcd(a,b),\qquad
\gcd(a,2b)=\gcd(a,b)\quad(a\text{ odd}),\qquad
\gcd(a,b)=\gcd(a,b-a)\quad(0<a\le b).
$$
\alttext(Common factors of two can be saved and restored. An unshared factor of two can be removed. Subtracting the smaller integer from the larger preserves the greatest common divisor.)

The first identity follows by dividing every shared divisor and both inputs by
two. In the second identity, every common divisor is odd because a is odd, so
the factor of two in b cannot matter. For subtraction, an integer divides both
a and b exactly when it divides both a and b minus a.

For nonzero inputs, save the shared power \(s=\min(v_2(a),v_2(b))\), computed
as the trailing-zero count of `a | b`. Make a odd. Each round strips all trailing
zeros from b, orders the odd operands so \(a\le b\), then sets \(b\leftarrow b-a\).
The invariant is that the original GCD equals \(2^s\gcd(a,b)\).
When b becomes zero the answer is \(a2^s\).

The library returns \(\gcd(0,b)=b\), \(\gcd(a,0)=a\), and
\(\gcd(0,0)=0\), without entering the loop. No trailing-zero count is used
as a shift on a zero word. Ordered subtraction avoids unsigned underflow;
the restored GCD fits because it cannot exceed either positive original input.

> Simple example: 54 and 24

The common power is \(s=1\). The first operand becomes 27. Stripping the three
trailing zero bits of 24 gives 3, so the first ordered odd pair is \((3,27)\).
Subtract to get \((3,24)\), strip three zero bits again, and subtract the equal
odd pair \((3,3)\). The terminal odd value is 3; restoring the saved factor gives 6.

```{cpp, file="figures/binary_gcd_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_gcd_trace(54,24);
  const auto first=trace.steps.front(),last=trace.steps.back();
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("input",{0,0},"54, 24\nSave common factor 2")
         .node("first",{6,0},std::format("Odd pair\n{}, {}",first.smaller_odd,first.larger_odd))
         .node("difference",{6,-3},std::format("After subtraction\n{}, {}",first.smaller_odd,first.difference))
         .node("equal",{0,-3},std::format("Odd pair\n{}, {}",last.smaller_odd,last.larger_odd))
         .node("zero",{0,-6},std::format("After subtraction\n{}, {}",last.smaller_odd,last.difference))
         .node("answer",{6,-6},std::format("Restore factor 2\nGCD = {}",trace.gcd))
         .edge("input","first","strip twos; order")
         .edge("first","difference","subtract")
         .edge("difference","equal","strip twos")
         .edge("equal","zero","subtract")
         .edge("zero","answer","stop");
  diagram.save_svg("build/binary_gcd_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/binary_gcd_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The native diagram labels use the recorded core states. The two subtraction rounds are separated from the saved common factor.)
\alttext(A six-stage diagram follows 54 and 24 through odd pairs three and 27, then three and three, terminating at three and zero and restoring the factor two to obtain six.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
int main() {
  const auto largest=std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max();
  std::print("| a | b | binary GCD | standard GCD | subtraction rounds |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [a,b]:std::vector<std::pair<std::uint64_t,std::uint64_t>>{
      {54,24},{1071,462},{0,0},{0,7},{9,0},{largest,largest},{largest,largest-1}}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_gcd_trace(a,b);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a,b,trace.gcd,std::gcd(a,b),trace.steps.size());
  }
}
```
