> The original example: 1071 and 462

Because 1071 is odd, no shared power of two is saved. The recorded post-subtraction
states are \((231,840),(105,126),(63,42),(21,42),(21,0)\).
Each row below reports the odd pair before subtraction as well as the resulting
difference. The number of removed twos refers to the incoming second operand
before any swap; it is not a factor removed from both inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/binary_gcd_trace.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_gcd_trace(1071,462);
  std::vector<double> round{0},a{double(trace.initial_odd_a)},b{462};
  std::print("| round | twos removed from incoming b | smaller odd | larger odd | difference |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& step:trace.steps) {
    round.push_back(round.size());
    a.push_back(step.smaller_odd); b.push_back(step.difference);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",round.size()-1,step.removed_twos,
               step.smaller_odd,step.larger_odd,step.difference);
  }
  std::print("\nGCD: {}; saved exponent: {}.\n",trace.gcd,trace.common_twos);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(round,a,"a after subtraction",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(round,a,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},6)
      .lines(round,b,"b after subtraction",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .points(round,b,"",dm::RGB{220,38,38},6)
      .title("Binary GCD of 1071 and 462")
      .x_label("subtraction rounds; zero is the initial state").y_label("operand value")
      .size(850,460).save_svg("build/binary_gcd_trace.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| round | twos removed from incoming b | smaller odd | larger odd | difference |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | 231 | 1071 | 840 |
| 2 | 3 | 105 | 231 | 126 |
| 3 | 1 | 63 | 105 | 42 |
| 4 | 1 | 21 | 63 | 42 |
| 5 | 1 | 21 | 21 | 0 |

GCD: 21; saved exponent: 0.
\image(figures/binary_gcd_trace.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The same original operand curves now come from the canonical trace API. An individual operand can rise after swapping; the pair's reduction is what matters.)
\alttext(The blue operand drops from 1071 to 21. The red operand initially rises from 462 to 840 after a swap and subtraction, then falls to zero in the fifth round.)

> Checking the round bound

Consider successive ordered odd pairs \((a,b)\), with \(a\le b\), before
subtraction. Unless this round terminates, \(b-a\) is positive and even. Before
the next subtraction it is replaced by \(c=(b-a)/2^k\) for some \(k\ge1\).
Ordering does not affect the product, and

$$
ac=\frac{a(b-a)}{2^k}<\frac{ab}{2}.
$$
\alttext(From one nonterminal odd pair to the next, its product decreases by more than a factor of two.)

For w-bit positive inputs the first product is less than \(2^{2w}\), while
every nonterminal odd product is at least one. There are at most \(2w\)
subtraction rounds. Each round uses a fixed number of word operations, so this
is \(O(w)\) word work. For multiprecision inputs, counting each arbitrary-width
shift or subtraction as constant work would be wrong; no such complexity claim
is made here.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/binary_gcd_rounds.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <random>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937_64 rng(20261106);
  std::vector<double> width,average,maximum,bound;
  std::print("| input bits | pairs | mean rounds | largest rounds | bound 2w | reference mismatches |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (unsigned bits:{4,8,12,16,24,32,48,64}) {
    const std::uint64_t mask=bits==64?~std::uint64_t{0}:(std::uint64_t{1}<<bits)-1;
    std::size_t total=0,most=0,mismatches=0;
    for (int trial=0;trial<1000;++trial) {
      const std::uint64_t a=rng()&mask,b=rng()&mask;
      const auto trace=dm::algorithms::binary_gcd_trace(a,b);
      total+=trace.steps.size(); most=std::max(most,trace.steps.size());
      mismatches+=trace.gcd!=std::gcd(a,b);
    }
    std::print("| {} | 1000 | {:.3f} | {} | {} | {} |\n",bits,total/1000.,most,2*bits,mismatches);
    if (mismatches || most>2*bits) return 1;
    width.push_back(bits); average.push_back(total/1000.); maximum.push_back(most); bound.push_back(2*bits);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(width,bound,"proved upper bound",dm::RGB{148,163,184},2)
      .lines(width,maximum,"largest of 1000 pairs",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .lines(width,average,"mean of 1000 pairs",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .title("Binary GCD subtraction rounds")
      .x_label("maximum input bit width").y_label("subtraction rounds").size(850,460)
      .save_svg("build/binary_gcd_rounds.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input bits | pairs | mean rounds | largest rounds | bound 2w | reference mismatches |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 4 | 1000 | 2.222 | 4 | 8 | 0 |
| 8 | 1000 | 5.136 | 8 | 16 | 0 |
| 12 | 1000 | 7.837 | 11 | 24 | 0 |
| 16 | 1000 | 10.714 | 16 | 32 | 0 |
| 24 | 1000 | 16.430 | 22 | 48 | 0 |
| 32 | 1000 | 22.013 | 28 | 64 | 0 |
| 48 | 1000 | 33.465 | 40 | 96 | 0 |
| 64 | 1000 | 44.617 | 55 | 128 | 0 |
\image(figures/binary_gcd_rounds.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The reproducible sweep counts subtraction rounds, not CPU cycles. Random samples support the checks but do not replace the product-shrink proof.)
\alttext(Mean and maximum observed rounds grow with bit width and remain below the straight-line bound of twice the bit width.)

> Conclusion

The shared core loop reproduces both hand calculations, handles zero without
entering the reduction loop, and agrees with the reference across generated
input widths. Native tests additionally compare it with a separate modulo-based
Euclidean implementation, exhaust all byte-sized operand pairs, and check
20,000 full-width random pairs and their trace invariants. No observed dataset
is needed for these exact arithmetic properties.

Avoiding division does not by itself establish a speed advantage: the machine's
instruction costs, branches, compiler and input distribution matter. The
unrecorded function uses constant auxiliary storage; the diagnostic trace owns
one record per subtraction. This implementation is limited to unsigned 64-bit
integers and does not provide a multiprecision or constant-time cryptographic API.

> References

- Stein, J. (1967). *Computational problems associated with Racah algebra*. Journal of Computational Physics, 1(3), 397–405. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9991(67)90047-2
